\details{
\strong{Method}

ANOSIM (Clarke 1993) ranks all \eqn{M = n(n-1)/2} distances, from 1 for
the smallest to \eqn{M} for the largest, giving tied distances the
average of their ranks. With \eqn{\bar{r}_B} the mean rank of the
distances between samples in different groups, and \eqn{\bar{r}_W} that
of the distances within groups,
\deqn{R = \frac{\bar{r}_B - \bar{r}_W}{M / 2}}
\eqn{R} is near 0 when the grouping explains nothing, and 1 when every
distance within groups is smaller than every distance between them.
Negative values mean samples are more alike between groups than within
them.

For each permutation, the samples' group labels are shuffled and
\eqn{R} is recomputed. The p-value is the proportion of permutations,
counting the observed one, with an \eqn{R} at least as large:
\eqn{(1 + \#\{R_{perm} \ge R\}) / (1 + permutations)}. As in \code{anosim()},
permuted statistics within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the observed one
count as ties.

ANOSIM tests for differences in location, but is also sensitive to
differences in spread (dispersion) between groups (Warton et al. 2012);
see also \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}.

\strong{Speed and memory}

The distances are ranked once, with a radix sort. Shuffling the labels
doesn't change how many pairs are within groups, nor the sum of all the
ranks, so each permutation needs only the sum of the ranks within groups:
one pass over the ranks, shared by many permutations at once, with no
\eqn{n \times n} matrix. Permutations run in parallel.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The permutations come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator,
so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and each permutation has a random number
stream of its own. P-values are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}. The random permutations differ from \code{anosim()}'s, so
p-values agree with it only up to sampling error, unless the same
\code{permutations} matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from vegan::anosim()}
\itemize{
\item \code{anosim()} here takes distances, not a community table: compute them
first with \code{\link[=beta_div]{beta_div()}} or a metric function such as \code{\link[=bray]{bray()}}.
\item The fields are named as in ecodive's other tests: \code{statistic} is named
\code{R}, \code{signif} is \code{p_value}, and the permuted statistics are attribute
\code{r_perm}. The full vectors of ranks and their classes (\code{dis.rank} and
\code{class.vec}), each as long as \code{dm}, are summarized as the mean rank of
each class, in field \code{ranks}.
\item \code{strata} permutes freely within strata. For other restricted designs,
pass a \code{permutations} matrix from \code{permute::shuffleSet()}.
\item When there are fewer possible permutations than requested, \code{vegan}
(via \code{permute}) runs each of them once instead. \code{anosim()} always draws
random permutations.
\item Negative distances are an error, where \code{vegan} warns.
}
}
\section{Pairwise comparisons}{

With \code{pairwise = TRUE}, each pair of groups is also tested on its own,
as q2-diversity's \code{beta_group_significance(pairwise=True)} does: the
distances are cut down to the two groups' samples, and the same test is
run on them, with the same number of \code{permutations}, the same \code{strata}
(cut down too), and the test's other arguments. The overall test is
unchanged.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Permutations:} Each pair has random permutations of its own, drawn
from \code{seed} and the pair's position in the table, never from the
number of \code{cpus}. So a pair's p-value is reproducible, but differs
(by sampling error) from that of the same test run by hand on the
pair's samples with the same \code{seed}. A supplied \code{permutations} matrix
is cut down to each pair's samples by following each permutation's
cycles: each of the pair's samples goes to the next of the pair's
samples on its cycle. That gives a permutation of the pair's samples,
uniformly distributed when the full one is, which keeps any
restriction the matrix has, such as permuting only within strata.
\item \strong{Multiple testing:} The pairs' permutation p-values are adjusted
together with \code{stats::p.adjust(method = p_adjust)}. The default,
Holm's method, controls the family-wise error rate whatever the
dependence between the pairs' tests. \code{'BH'} controls the false
discovery rate instead, as q2-diversity's q-values do. The raw
p-values are kept as well.
\item \strong{Pairs that can't be tested:} A pair the test can't be run on, such
as two groups of one sample each, gets \code{NA}s, with a warning, rather
than an error. A test's other warnings for a pair, such as all of its
distances being 0, are passed on, naming the pair.
}

The \code{pairwise} field of the result is a data frame with one row per pair
of groups, named like \code{"a-b"}, in the order of the groups' factor levels
(sorted values, for other vectors). Its columns are the test's
statistics for the pair, \code{z}, \code{n} (the pair's number of samples),
\code{n_perm}, \code{p_value} (unadjusted) and \code{p_adjusted}. The \code{p_adjust} field
names the method, and \code{print()} shows both.
}

\examples{
    # 30 samples in three treatment groups. The 'high' group has more
    # of the first five genera.
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(30 * 20, 5), 30, 20)
    rownames(counts) <- paste0('S', 1:30)
    meta <- data.frame(
      row.names = rownames(counts),
      treatment = rep(c('ctrl', 'low', 'high'), each = 10),
      site      = rep(c('A', 'B'), 15) )
    high <- meta$treatment == 'high'
    counts[high, 1:5] <- counts[high, 1:5] + 5

    dm <- bray(counts)

    # Are samples more alike within treatment groups than between them?
    res <- anosim(dm, 'treatment', meta)
    res
    plot(res)

    # The same, with the groups as a vector.
    anosim(dm, meta$treatment)

    # Permuting treatment labels only within each site.
    anosim(dm, 'treatment', meta, strata = 'site')

    # Mean rank of the distances between and within groups.
    anosim(dm, 'treatment', meta)$ranks

    # Each pair of treatments, with Holm-adjusted p-values.
    anosim(dm, 'treatment', meta, pairwise = TRUE)$pairwise

}
